## The isolation rule (the owner's)

> "make sure when you merge the two you have the codes separated completely
> so none interact with each other and can break one another ever."

The dongle and the EXO app are served from the same folder (same origin) but
are two apps:

- **No shared file.** Nothing under `dongle/` loads a file outside it, and no
  EXO page loads anything under it. The only links are the device list tile
  (`devices.js`, `href: 'dongle/dongle.html'`) and the dongle's way back
  (`../devices.html?pick=1`). The palette in `css/zs-dongle-theme.css` is a
  COPY of the EXO's 8K flat tokens (`zs-flat.css`, D259), not a link, and
  `js/zs-ble-cap.js` is a frozen copy of the EXO's Capacitor Bluetooth
  polyfill (the calibrator page loads it inside the phone app).
- **No shared global.** The two never define the same `window` name.
- **No shared storage.** Every localStorage / sessionStorage key the dongle
  uses starts `zsdongle:`. The EXO app never uses that prefix and its factory
  reset (`/^zs[-.]/`) cannot match it. Neither side calls `clear()`, opens
  IndexedDB or registers a service worker. `js/zs-dongle-storage.js` (loaded
  first by both pages) moves the old phone app's un-prefixed keys
  (`zsDongle`, `zsCalibratorLink`, `zsOtaSession:*`, `zsDongleSlotNames:*`,
  the calibrator's `finetuneHistory`, `changes_*`, ...) into the prefix once,
  so a phone that ran the old app keeps its remembered dongle, script names,
  calibration history and an interrupted update.
- **No shared Bluetooth connection.** Each app opens its own. The dongle
  disconnects when you leave it for the device list (its back button, and the
  calibrator's back key); inside the dongle, dongle.html -> calibrator.html
  keeps the link on purpose (the calibrator adopts it, hid-ble.js). The EXO
  app's link is its own (zs-ble.js). Inside the phone app both use the one
  native Capacitor BLE plugin, but only one of the two pages is ever loaded,
  and a page's plugin listeners die with it.

`dashboard/tools/test-dongle-isolation.js` (run by `run-all.js`) fails the
build if any of that is broken.

## Platforms

- **Desktop exe** (Edge `--app`): real Web Bluetooth. Served by
  `dashboard/server.js` like every public file; `pkg` bundles `public/**/*`.
  The WiFi calibrator: the exe joins `ZeroStick-Calibrator` ITSELF after a
  tap on Connect (D273): `app-connect.js` calls the exe's own routes
  `/zs/wifi/status|join|restore|location-settings` (`dashboard/src/wifi.js`:
  netsh, the old desktop app's open profile + connect; networksetup on a
  Mac; that one SSID only) and puts the PC back on its own network when the
  person leaves. These are HTTP routes on the server that serves the page,
  not files, so the isolation test allows exactly `/zs/wifi/<name>`. Opened
  in a plain browser (no such routes), step 3 says to join the network in
  the WiFi settings.
- **Phone app** (Capacitor, `com.zerostick.calibrator`): `hid-ble.js` talks to
  the native BLE plugin directly, so a remembered dongle reconnects silently.
  The WiFi calibrator (the ZeroStick Calibrator, D276) is found with Search
  and joined with the
  `@falconeta/capacitor-wifi-connect` plugin and reaches `http://192.168.4.1`
  (cleartext allowed for that address only, network_security_config).
- **Testing without hardware:** `dongle.html?mock=1` (`js/zs-dongle-mock*.js`)
  is a simulated dongle; `?mock=off` turns it off. `theme-shots.js --pages`
  uses it for the screenshots.

## The calibrator page's top bar and Connect screen (D273)

`calibrator.html` starts with the dongle screens' top bar (`#zsbar`, the
back key top left and "Calibrator"; `js/zs-cal-bar.js`): back goes to
`dongle.html` when opened from the dongle hub (`?from=dongle`, the
Bluetooth link kept), otherwise to the device list after letting go of a
Bluetooth dongle and (exe) restoring the PC's WiFi. The floating bottom-left
home / back buttons were removed. The Connect screen under it is
`dongle.html`'s Connect view (photo, title, three numbered steps, note,
docked Connect button, progress line, error cards), drawn by
`js/app-connect.js` and styled by `css/zs-cal-connect.css` (rules copied from
`zs-dongle.css`, scoped, because Bootstrap owns the bare elements here).
`tools/test-calibrator-connect.js` covers it.

## The WiFi calibrator (the store app's behaviour, kept)

The calibrator in the Play Store today is **versionCode 1 / "1.0"**. The repo
was never re-versioned after that upload, so the exact commit is not tagged;
the upload happened on 2026-09-04 (the Play assets were committed at 15:36 and
the release build output is dated 16:51), which matches **4b05111** ("Name the
calibrator card..."), the last commit of that day. Between 4b05111 and 2bba734
the WiFi path changed only by addition: `app-connect.js` asks once "which
calibrator do you have" (or is told by the dongle screen), and with
"wifi" chosen runs the store build's join / check `/info` / reconnect
sequence byte for byte; `hid-proxy.js` uses Bluetooth only when the Bluetooth
dongle is connected and otherwise the store build's `/hid/*` calls; the
calibrator's WiFi hints are only hidden for Bluetooth. Nothing in the WiFi
code was changed in this move. The board's firmware is never updated (owner
rule), so the app side must keep speaking to it exactly like this.
D273 redrew the screen and added the PC exe's join; what is said to the board
did not change (the plugin's `connect({ssid, saveNetwork:true})`, `/info`
must say `zerostick-calibrator`, the 4 s watchdog with three misses). The
only new limit: a ping waits 4 s at most in a browser too, as the app's
native request always did.

## The ZeroStick Calibrator: Search -> pick -> connect -> straight in (D276)

The WiFi calibrator is called the **ZeroStick Calibrator** everywhere a
person reads it (device list tile, page title, Connect screen). Its WiFi
name (SSID) is fixed in its never-updated firmware as
`ZeroStick-Calibrator` (hyphen): `app-connect.js` keeps `WIFI_NAME` for
joining and `SHOW_NAME` for showing.

- **Android:** Search -> permission (one line why) -> `getSSIDs()` scan ->
  a list of ONLY calibrators (others counted, never named) -> tap -> the
  unchanged `connect()` -> `/info` -> in. None found: Search again or
  "Join ZeroStick Calibrator anyway". The phone app's plugin patch
  (`patches/@falconeta+capacitor-wifi-connect+8.0.0.patch`) returns the last
  scan when Android throttles a new one (4 scans / 2 min).
- **iPhone:** no app may list WiFi; Search goes straight to the system's
  "Join ZeroStick-Calibrator?" prompt, then in.
- **PC exe:** Search -> `GET /zs/wifi/scan` (netsh, read only, whitelist) ->
  the same list -> the D273 join. A Mac joins directly.
- **On open** nothing is joined: already on the calibrator = straight in,
  otherwise the screen waits for Search. After the app joined once, a drop
  rejoins by itself (the 4 s watchdog, three misses), as before.

### Checklist with the ZeroStick Calibrator (owner, on a real phone)

1. Plug the calibrator into the PS5 or a USB charger, controller into it;
   wait for the blue light (it broadcasts the open network
   `ZeroStick-Calibrator`).
2. Open the ZeroStick app -> the device list -> tap **ZeroStick Calibrator**.
3. "Connect your ZeroStick Calibrator": tap **Search**.
   - Android: allow Location / Nearby devices when asked; the list shows ONE
     row, **ZeroStick Calibrator**; tap it; accept the system's
     "connect to ZeroStick-Calibrator" prompt if shown.
   - iPhone: the system asks "Join ZeroStick-Calibrator?"; tap **Join**.
4. "Checking the calibrator", "Connected", then the calibrator page opens by
   itself (it checks `http://192.168.4.1/info` says `zerostick-calibrator`).
5. Run a range and centre calibration on one stick, save it.
6. Pull the calibrator's power a few seconds and plug it back: the app shows
   the Connect screen and rejoins by itself (no new Search).
7. Android: tap Search 5+ times quickly; it should still find it (the patch).
   With the calibrator unplugged: "No calibrator found yet" + Join anyway.
8. Tap the back key (top left): back on the device list.

With the new Bluetooth dongle instead: on the calibrator screen tap
**I have the new calibrator (it's all black)**.

## Open items (not done in the move)

- **Keep-awake during firmware updates**: `zs-fw.js` uses the browser
  `navigator.wakeLock`; a native keep-awake plugin needs a package.json change
  in the phone app, still open.
- **Production signing key** for the dongle firmware images (`fw/`, Ed25519)
  is still open on the DSE side. `zs-fw.js` checks size, sha256 and crc32
  against `fw/manifest.json` (made by the DSE repo's `make_release.py`). The
  phone app's `tools/sync-dse-fw.js` still copies release firmware into the
  phone repo's old `www/fw/`; it must be pointed at THIS folder's `fw/`
  (`dashboard/public/dongle/fw/`) so new images reach both apps.
- The phone app's old `www/js/` copies are unused and can be deleted later.
